<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Mersey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Mersey is a river of north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> through a wide estuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>. Its name means 'boundary river', and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the frontier between Northumbria and Mercia. The estuary later became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s great ports and the heart of the industrial north-west.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-mersey.jpg" alt="River Mersey" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rept0n1x · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liverpool_skyline_from_the_Mersey_Ferry_-_2012-05-27.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Mersey is 69 miles (111 km) long. It is now regarded as beginning at the confluence of the River Tame and the River Goyt in the centre of Stockport, though older definitions and maps place its start near Marple, where the Etherow meets the Goyt. It flows west through south Manchester and, near Irlam Locks, into the Manchester Ship Canal, which obliterated its old course for part of the way. After leaving the canal it runs to Warrington and through the Runcorn Gap between Runcorn and Widnes.</p><p>Below it a large estuary opens, 3 miles (4.8 km) across at its widest near Ellesmere Port, then narrows to 0.7 miles (1.1 km) between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Birkenhead before it empties into Liverpool Bay. Its tidal range is the second highest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, after the Severn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> words mǣres, of a boundary, and ēa, a river, and may refer to its having been the border between the kingdoms of Mercia and Northumbria. For centuries it has formed part of the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>. Before the Anglo-Saxon conquest the river had a Celtic name, recorded by Ptolemy as Seteia, as the southern border of the Setantii tribe.</p><p>The lowest bridging point was at Warrington, where there has been a bridge since medieval times. Monks from Birkenhead Priory introduced the first ferry across the estuary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and sheltered travellers at the priory in bad weather.</p><p>Birkenhead grew quickly in the 19th century after the paddle steamer Elizabeth of 1815, with docks, shipbuilding, flour milling and soap manufacture. In 1836 Captain William Gill of the Isle of Man Steam Packet Company charted a safe channel, the Victoria Channel, through the estuary. The Mersey Railway tunnel between Birkenhead and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> opened in 1886, and the Queensway Tunnel in 1934.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>'s docks stretch over 7.5 miles (12.1 km). Industrialisation badly polluted the river, and in 1985 the Mersey Basin Campaign began to improve water quality; in 2002 oxygen levels able to support fish were recorded along its whole length for the first time since industry began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the conurbation on both sides of the estuary is known as Merseyside. The Mersey Ferry still runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> to Birkenhead and Seacombe. The wetlands of the estuary are a Ramsar site. The river gave its name to Merseybeat, developed by Liverpool bands including the Beatles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Mersey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
